PREAMBLE — CODE FOR THOSE WHO BUILD, NOT THOSE WHO OPPRESS

Software is produced by human labor, collective knowledge, shared infrastructure,
and generations of people who freely passed ideas to one another. This work is
published in the conviction that technology should increase ordinary people's
freedom, dignity, solidarity, and capacity to act together. Its author takes a
communist position: the fruits of collective labor should serve social needs,
not become unpaid fuel for institutions whose business is domination, coercion,
surveillance, incarceration, or organized violence. No worker should have to
surrender the moral meaning of their labor merely because a machine can execute it.

This license therefore makes a deliberate distinction. Individuals may learn,
create, experiment, and share. Organizations that contribute real work to the
commons earn the same freedom. Other companies must ask, but silence is not a
veto: after fifteen days without an express decision, permission is automatic.
Institutions of repression and organized reaction do not receive a license.
The source code is offered in solidarity, not in political neutrality. This is
both a copyright notice and a declaration that our labor is not theirs to command.

1. DEFINITIONS

1.1. “Software” means the original source code, documentation, and other
copyrightable material expressly distributed by a rights holder under TSAL-1.0,
including covered modifications. It does not include separately licensed
third-party material merely bundled with, linked to, or referenced by the Software.

1.2. “Licensor” means each copyright holder who releases their contribution
under TSAL-1.0, or a person authorized to license that contribution.

1.3. “Individual” means a natural person acting in a personal capacity and not
for, on behalf of, or for the benefit of an organization in connection with the
Software. An employee acting for an employer is not an Individual for that use.

1.4. “Organization” means any corporation, company, association, foundation,
nonprofit, public agency, political party, collective, or other legal or factual
body, whether incorporated or not. “Company” means an Organization that carries
on an economic or professional business activity, regardless of profit motive.

1.5. “Excluded Party” means a person or Organization within Section 3. “Covered
Use” includes running, accessing, hosting, integrating, modifying, reproducing,
distributing, or otherwise exploiting the Software, whether directly or through
a contractor, employee, agent, proxy, subsidiary, or controlled entity.

1.6. “Qualifying Pull Request” means a distinct, good-faith GitHub pull request
proposing a substantive improvement to the Software or its documentation or
tests; submitted to the Software's official upstream repository; and accepted
and merged by its maintainers. Duplicate, spam, purely cosmetic, automatically
generated without meaningful review, or reverted-for-abuse submissions do not
qualify. A contribution by a natural person counts for an Organization only if
that person's contribution was expressly identified in the pull request as
being made on the Organization's behalf. The two pull requests must be separate.

2. CONDITIONAL GRANT OF RIGHTS

2.1. Subject to Sections 3–8, each Licensor grants each eligible recipient a
non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free copyright license to use, execute,
reproduce, study, modify, prepare derivative works from, publicly display,
make available, and distribute the Software in source or executable form.
No trademark or patent license is granted except as required by applicable law.

2.2. Individuals who are not Excluded Parties may exercise these rights without
requesting consent, including for learning, research, artistic creation,
hobby projects, and their own independent work. An Individual may not use this
provision as a nominal stand-in for an Organization's Covered Use.

2.3. An Organization that is not an Excluded Party may exercise these rights
without requesting consent if at least two Qualifying Pull Requests have been
accepted and merged on its behalf before its Covered Use begins. Once earned,
this qualification applies to the Organization's continuing use of the Software,
provided it remains eligible and complies with this License.

2.4. An Organization that does not qualify under Section 2.3 must obtain the
permission described in Section 4 before undertaking Covered Use. Such
permission is royalty-free under this License unless the parties separately
agree otherwise in writing.

2.5. Neither contribution, silence, payment, a contractual arrangement, nor
purported permission from an intermediary can override Section 3. Only a
separate, express license executed by all relevant rights holders could confer
rights otherwise unavailable under this License.

3. ABSOLUTE EXCLUSIONS

No license is granted to the following parties, or to any person or entity
knowingly undertaking Covered Use on their behalf or for their operational
benefit:

(a) Armed forces, militaries, military commands, and military intelligence units.
(b) Paramilitary formations, militias organized for coercive violence, and
    private military organizations.
(c) Police departments, law-enforcement agencies, and organizations carrying
    out policing powers.
(d) Prison and detention operators, prison-management and incarceration
    businesses, and prison or detention officials in connection with their
    official work or an institutional deployment.
(e) Manufacturers, developers, and suppliers of weapons, ammunition, or
    dedicated weapons systems when acting in that business.
(f) Intelligence, espionage, covert-operations, and secret-service agencies.
(g) Religious-extremist organizations that organize, advocate, or carry out
    violence, persecution, or coercive denial of fundamental rights in the name
    of religion; religious belief or affiliation alone is not an exclusion.
(h) Far-right or extreme-right political parties, groups, and organizations
    that advocate fascism, neo-Nazism, ethnic supremacy, or systematic denial
    of equal civil or political rights on grounds of identity. Mere conservative
    views, individual beliefs, or political disagreement alone are insufficient.
(i) Organizations whose substantial organized purpose is to prohibit,
    criminalize, or obstruct access to voluntary abortion (“anti-abortion
    organizations”); an individual's personal beliefs alone are not an exclusion.
(j) Drug cartels and organizations materially engaged in organized illicit
    drug trafficking.
(k) Businesses that provide surveillance of people, including commercial
    tracking, invasive monitoring, or surveillance services, whether physical
    or digital.
(l) Mercenary groups, companies, brokers, and organizations providing armed
    mercenary services.
(m) Businesses providing private security, guarding, or contracted physical
    or digital security services for others.

The classification depends on demonstrable conduct, declared objectives,
operations, or services, rather than a Licensor's uncommunicated opinion.
An otherwise eligible individual is not excluded solely because of employment,
family connection, religion, or political opinion; however, Covered Use for an
Excluded Party remains prohibited. Excluded Parties may not avoid this Section
by using subsidiaries, contractors, intermediaries, personal accounts, or
otherwise eligible Organizations.

4. PERMISSION BY REQUEST OR FIFTEEN-DAY SILENCE

4.1. An Organization needing permission under Section 2.4 may send a written
request to the License contact prominently identified at the top of the
repository's LICENSE file. The request must identify the Organization by its
actual name, provide a reply address, identify the repository, and state that
permission under TSAL-1.0 is requested. No description of business secrets,
planned features, or proprietary use is required.

4.2. The request is received when it reaches the published contact address
without a delivery-failure notice. The applicant should preserve a dated copy
and reasonable delivery evidence. If no functioning License contact is
published, automatic permission by silence is unavailable until the Licensor
publishes such a contact; the alternative contribution route in Section 2.3
remains available.

4.3. The Licensor may grant or deny the request in an express written response
received by the applicant during the following fifteen consecutive calendar
days (360 hours). An acknowledgement or request for information that neither
grants nor denies permission does not stop this period. Permission takes effect
automatically upon expiry of that period if no express grant or denial has
reached the applicant. Silence is itself an advance, conditional grant under
this public License, not a presumed private contract or a waiver of Section 3.

4.4. An express denial received within that period bars permission by silence
for the same Organization and Software; repeated substantially identical
requests do not erase the denial. A later express written grant may supersede
it. Permission obtained by an express grant or by silence continues without
renewal while the Organization remains eligible and compliant. A purported
denial arriving after automatic permission has vested does not retroactively
revoke compliant use.

5. DISTRIBUTION AND RECIPIENT RIGHTS

5.1. Every source or executable copy distributed must retain this License,
the copyright notices, and a notice identifying material modifications.
When distributing a modified executable, the distributor must also make the
corresponding modified source code available to recipients under TSAL-1.0 at
no more than the reasonable cost of transfer.

5.2. Modified versions incorporating Software-covered material must remain
subject to TSAL-1.0 for that covered material. Original independent material
and separately licensed third-party components retain their own applicable
rights; this License does not purport to relicense material the Licensor does
not own.

5.3. Distribution does not automatically qualify a recipient. Each recipient
must independently satisfy the eligibility requirements in Sections 2–4 and
must comply with Section 3 before exercising rights under this License.
Distributors may not imply that the Software is available under an unrestricted
open-source license.

6. CONTRIBUTIONS

A contribution intentionally submitted for inclusion in the official repository
and expressly accepted under TSAL-1.0 is licensed by its contributor under
TSAL-1.0. Contribution does not transfer copyright ownership. A maintainer
cannot grant rights to third-party contributions under terms broader than those
authorized by their respective copyright holders.

7. TERMINATION AND REINSTATEMENT

Rights under this License automatically terminate for a recipient that becomes
an Excluded Party or materially violates its terms. For a curable breach other
than a Section 3 exclusion, rights are reinstated prospectively if the breach
is cured within thirty days after written notice from a relevant Licensor.
Termination does not retroactively invalidate compliant copies or grants
previously received by independent eligible recipients.

